  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1221 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nUpper level ridge continues to influence conditions across the \narea today with light southerly flow and WAA supporting seasonably\nwarm temperatures in the upper 50s to low 60s. Water vapor \nimagery shows subtle shortwave feature crossing the CWA with the \nonly impacts being some mid to high level cloud cover moving over \nwestern SD. After a mild night, return flow and WAA ramps up over \nthe region tomorrow with highs climbing into the upper 70s to low \n80s across the western SD plains and northeastern WY. Rapid City \nitself could reach the low 80s, though high clouds in the \nafternoon could keep things slightly cooler. Regardless, it should\nbe an unseasonably warm day across the area.\n\nNorthern stream shortwave moves through the Northern Plains late \nFriday. Southwest winds will pick up through the day Friday ahead \nof the attendant sfc cold front with gusts of 30-40 mph over much \nof the western SD plains and northeastern WY. Strong WAA through \nthe day Friday will support highs well above average (70s to 80s \nacross much of the western SD plains into northeastern WY). Rapid \nCity could break a daily record with forecast highs of 83 \n(previous records were 82 and 80 in downtown Rapid City and at our\nairport respectively). The warm, dry, and windy conditions will \nsupport elevated to near critical fire wx concerns Friday \nafternoon. See Fire Wx Discussion for more details.\n\nCurrent deterministic model guidance shows front will push \nthrough the region late Friday night through early Saturday \nmorning with winds shifting to the northwest behind the front. \nWinds will remain gusty through the overnight hours into Saturday \nmorning with most recent NAM runs showing modest pressure rises of\n3-5 mb early Saturday morning. Strong CAA and 850-700mb winds of \n30-40KT will support gusty northwest winds, especially over \nnorthwestern SD.\n\nUpper level low over the desert SW will eject over the Central \nPlains by Sunday. As this low lifts northeastward and grazes the \nCWA, we could get a round of precip Sunday afternoon into Sunday \nevening. All deterministic runs and ensembles have been trending \nupwards in terms of QPF over the area Sunday. As for whether this \nQPF will be in the form of rain or snow, right now it's looking \nlike mostly rain over the lower elevations of the plains with the \nhigher elevations in the Black Hills being cold enough for snow. \nIn terms of snow: The most recent GFS deterministic runs are the \nmost aggressive with this system, showing several inches of \naccumulating snowfall over the Black Hills and portions of \nsouthwestern SD and the western SD plains. The Euro and Canadian \nmodels are a bit more conservative and show little, if any, snow \naccumulation. Given the recent warm temps and timing of snowfall \n(looking like this should be in the afternoon/evening hours). All \nof this to say: At this time it's not looking likely that we'd see\nmuch accumulation except for in the highest elevations of the \nBlack Hills.",
